        },
        {
          "text": "In a strict pivotal category these deﬁnitions simplify to:",
          "bbox": [
            72.0,
            377.91,
            363.96,
            389.87
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "CMR10"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            11.96
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "trL(f) = ǫ(a∗)(1 ⊗f)ǫ(a∗)∗",
          "bbox": [
            215.88,
            394.22,
            358.66,
            410.05
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "CMMI10",
            "CMMI7",
            "CMR10",
            "CMSY10",
            "CMSY7"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            8.37,
            11.96
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "trR(f) = ǫ(a)(f ⊗1)ǫ(a)∗",
          "bbox": [
            215.16,
            412.82,
            347.74,
            428.65
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "CMMI10",
            "CMMI7",
            "CMR10",
            "CMSY10",
            "CMSY7"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            8.37,
            11.96
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "These are called trace maps because they satisfy trL(fg) = trL(gf) and trR(fg) =",
          "bbox": [
            72.0,
            435.87,
            502.38,
            448.57
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "CMMI10",
            "CMMI7",
            "CMR10"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            8.37,
            11.96
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "trR(gf).",
          "bbox": [
            72.0,
            450.27,
            115.28,
            462.97
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "CMMI10",
            "CMMI7",
            "CMR10"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            8.37,
            11.96
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Deﬁnition 2.5. A pivotal category is spherical if, for all objects a and all mor-",
          "bbox": [
            72.0,
            469.32,
            502.36,
            481.67
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "CMBX10",
            "CMMI10",
            "CMR10"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            11.96
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "phisms f: a →a,",
          "bbox": [
            72.0,
            484.11,
            159.08,
            496.07
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "CMMI10",
            "CMR10",
            "CMSY10"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            11.96
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "trL(f) = trR(f).",
          "bbox": [
            244.32,
            498.39,
            330.2,
            511.09
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "CMMI10",
            "CMMI7",
            "CMR10"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            8.37,
            11.96
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "An equivalent condition is that trL(f) = trL(f ∗), for all f: a →a. Also, in a",
          "bbox": [
            86.4,
            519.62,
            502.43,
            534.85
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "CMMI10",
            "CMMI7",
            "CMR10",
            "CMSY10",
            "CMSY7"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            8.37,
            11.96
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "spherical category, trL(f ⊗g) = trL(f). trL(g) (where the product is in End(e)) for",
          "bbox": [
            72.0,
            536.43,
            502.34,
            549.13
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "CMMI10",
            "CMMI7",
            "CMR10",
            "CMSY10"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            8.37,
            11.96
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "all f: a →a and all g: b →b.",
          "bbox": [
            72.0,
            550.83,
            219.81,
            562.79
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "CMMI10",
            "CMR10",
            "CMSY10"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            11.96
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "In the rest of this paper we study additive spherical categories. This means that",
          "bbox": [
            86.4,
            565.11,
            502.41,
            577.07
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "CMR10"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            11.96
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "each Hom set is a ﬁnitely generated abelian group; and that the data deﬁning the",
          "bbox": [
            72.0,
            579.51,
            502.38,
            591.47
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "CMR10"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            11.96
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "spherical structure is compatible with the additive structure. This means that ⊗is",
          "bbox": [
            72.0,
            593.79,
            502.48,
            605.75
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "CMR10",
            "CMSY10"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            11.96
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Z-bilinear, that ∗is Z-linear and all components of all the natural transformations",
          "bbox": [
            72.0,
            605.52,
            502.36,
            620.15
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "CMR10",
            "CMSY10",
            "MSBM10"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            11.96
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "in the pivotal data are multilinear over Z.",
          "bbox": [
            72.0,
            619.92,
            291.2,
            634.55
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "CMR10",
            "MSBM10"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            11.96
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "In any additive monoidal category End(e) is a commutative ring (see [Kelly and",
          "bbox": [
            86.4,
            636.87,
            502.37,
            648.83
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "CMMI10",
            "CMR10"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            11.96
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Laplaza 1980]) and we denote this ring by F. In particular the trace map, trL,",
          "bbox": [
            72.0,
            648.6,
            502.41,
            663.97
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "CMMI7",
            "CMR10",
            "MSBM10"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            8.37,
            11.96
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "takes values in this ring. It follows that an additive monoidal category is F-linear,",
          "bbox": [
            72.0,
            662.88,
            502.41,
            677.51
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "CMR10",
            "MSBM10"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            11.96
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "by using the structure maps of λ and ρ, and that if the category is pivotal then the",
          "bbox": [
            72.0,
            679.95,
            502.41,
            691.91
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "CMMI10",
            "CMR10"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            11.96
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "data deﬁning the pivotal structure is F-linear.",
          "bbox": [
            72.0,
            691.56,
            311.97,
            706.19
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "CMR10",
            "MSBM10"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            11.96
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "The main examples of additive spherical categories arise as the category of rep-",
          "bbox": [
            86.4,
            708.63,
            502.35,
            720.59
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "CMR10"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            11.96
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "resentations of a Hopf algebra with some additional structure. This is discussed in",
          "bbox": [
            72.0,
            722.91,
            502.48,
            734.87
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "CMR10"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            11.96
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "detail in the next section.",
          "bbox": [
            72.0,
